None of these connections have been proven, as far as I know.  The problem
with the argument that alcoholism is genetically influenced is that groups
that are genetically similar, like Eskimos and Asians, have opposite
alcohol pathologies.  See "The Diseasing of America," by Stanton Peele,
on this.  Sexual orientation has been found connected not to genetics but
to pre-natal hormonal exposure.  Males exposed to more estrogen (or less
testosterone, I'm not sure which) during a certain period of pre-natal
development tend to be more attracted to other males; females exposed to
more testosterone (or less estrogen) tend to be more attracted to other
females.  Unfortunately, my source from this is a book that hasn't been
published in the USA yet: "The Sex-Y Factor," by Frances Kendall.  I read
the edition which's just been published in South Africa, where she's from.

>(An argument that, in retrospect, strikes me as very similar to
