>This
>is the fundamental problem I have with all those studies which
>attempt to show certain abilities are sex linked/influenced.  I
>honestly don't *care* which sex is more apt on average with math.

Kendall's book originated in a disagreement she had with Walter Williams
(an African-American libertarian economist) about the differences in the
math achievement of boys and girls.  She thought that if they existed that
they were the result of nurture.  She changed her mind after doing some
research.  She says that the best girls don't even do as well as the worst
boys in math achievement distribution curves.

The relevance of this isn't that girls shouldn't try to learn math, but
that it's to be expected that there will be more men than women whose
occupations require math - as a result of biology, not ideology.  Disparate
distribution of the sexes in occupations doesn't have to be because of
patriarchal oppression, and essentially isn't.  Accidental patriarchal
oppression exists and does contribute, but even if this is eradicated,
the disparate distribution will continue to exist.

Kendall also points out that the studies don't find only that men do
better than women at some things.  They also find that women to better than
men at others.

