Subject: DIET: Vegans and "The Kosher People"

About a week and a half ago I was at my aunt Anita's house
visiting, when I asked how Anne (my cousin) was doing. Anita
said she was off in Chicago with Neil visiting with Neil's  
folks; she said it was a relief not to have to cook for the
Kosher People for a change.

Note to spectators: all I know at this point is that Neil is
jewish; I don't really know anything else, and to date all of
my previous jewish friends have played fast and loose with the
kosher laws (I think to save their life: I suspect there are gangs
of people out there hunting down and killing S. Louisianians who
don't like shellfish; I run into severe problems just because I don't
like _boiled_ seafood).

Anyway, I asked, basically: what do you mean? I didn't think that
kosher food was all that difficult to prepare, etc... and that of
the normal diet Anita usually fixed, it would mean an exclusion of
mainly pork and shellfish. (Note: Anita is not only a good cook,
who usually cooks food much healthier than my mom does, but is
also a medtech and a health food nut. She is neither vegan nor
vegitarian).

She said that that wasn't the case. Neil apparently belonged to
some subsect where the food not only had to be physically kosher
but legally kosher as well. One of the basic effects was that
what meat _was_ allowed, was between 2 and 3 times the cost of said
meat for gentiles. PLUS: a lot of the non-shellfish seafood around
here, like catfish, was not kosher due to the lack of pproper
scales(!). So when Anne and Neil were in, she was limited to
vegetable dishes with maybe a little chicken (the kosher beef was
too expensive).

Getting to the question: is vegetarianism "easier" to adopt if one
was raised in a Jewish sect that practiced  very strict dietary
discipline? It looks to me like some of them are halfway there.

