To my knowledge (no refs, sorry) this is not the case, as I mentioned
last time this came up.  Longitudinal studies on different fat levels
in diet do not show _overall_ mortality differences.
1)   High-fat folks have higher deaths rates from heart disease
     (saturated fats) and cancer (unsaturated fats).
2)   Low-fat folks have higher death rates from violence (murder _and_
     suicide), accidents and the like.
Which suggests that the present dietary fat levels are not as sub-
optimal as some suggest.  For a possible causative link note that the
brain contains loads of cholesterol (and other fats), so a low fat diet
could cause sub-optimal behaviour patterns.

