>Why?

I dislike efforts to demonstrate the nature side of this debate
(behavior/ability/etc.) for a couple of reasons.  One, I think
that it *won't* lead to more tolerance, but may be used in some
rather horrible ways (selective abortion, coercive sterilization, etc.).
Two, I think that when people believe that, by nature/genetic heritage/etc.
(something beyond their control), they are not good at something (or
even if they believe they belong to a population which tends to be less able
to do something), they will tend to not bother to try.  Furthermore,
they will be discouraged from trying.  In the specific case of females
and math, I was very lucky to have a lot of teachers who explicitly
believed either that (a) all those studies about girls aren't good
at math are bogus or (b) it doesn't matter, girls should still be
encouraged to work hard at and do well in mathematics and scientific
disciplines, even if this isn't where their aptitude lies, because anything
else is condemning them to low wages.  But for every teacher of that
stripe, I've heard of a two or three others (and not all of mine were 
particularly enlightened -- one of my math teachers who also taught my high 
school programming classes did just about everything in his power to run
me out of those programming classes on account of women aren't logical.
This despite the fact that I understood the material faster and better
than the other -- all male -- members of the class and was demonstrably
better at applying it.) who, when faced with a struggling female student,
reassured her that it wasn't her fault, "girls aren't good at math",
and let her pass, on condition she didn't take the next class in the sequence.
At least a couple had parents who weren't about to accept that; 
I wound up tutoring those girls.

Which is a horribly roundabout way of explaining what I meant when 
I said such knowledge (whether accurate or not) is something I'm
not in any hurry to have around.  In practice, "girls are bad at
math" is a self-fulfilling prophecy.

(Please don't take this wrong.  I am not suggesting such research
should be terminated or anyting like that. I just don't happen
to like it very much.)

This is not to say that *everyone* responds to this information in
this way.  The CS department at the UW seems to be quite concerned
about the ratio of female students to male -- it has been declining
over the last few years (and that article Communications ran a while
back suggests that it isn't a local phenomenon).  It can be argued
that if you're having a hard time getting enough people that
are good enough in a particular discipline, and you happen to
notice a really skewed sex distribution, it might be worthwhile actively
recruiting in the underrepresented population -- no telling what
genius lurks there, untapped (ditto for any other discernible population,
not just by sex).

>She says that the best girls don't even do as well as the worst
>boys in math achievement distribution curves.

Yow.  I think I'd like a reference for this research.  I have a hard
time believing it (esp. with counterexamples like my sister
and myself running about -- we're no Hypatias, but we're reasonably
dab hands).

If, on the other hand, I could rely on most people to take this
stance:

>The relevance of this isn't that girls shouldn't try to learn math, but
>that it's to be expected that there will be more men than women whose
>occupations require math - as a result of biology, not ideology.  Disparate
>distribution of the sexes in occupations doesn't have to be because of
>patriarchal oppression, and essentially isn't.  Accidental patriarchal
>oppression exists and does contribute, but even if this is eradicated,
>the disparate distribution will continue to exist.

my worries would all away.  :-)

>David Friedman told me when I visited him in Chicago that Heinlein figured
>out a libertarian eugenics method in theory: have sperm and ova that contain
>bad genes taken out of the pool before conception.

Slick solution -- had not heard this before, hadn't thought of this
before, but it's retrospectively obvious.

>Yet another female list-reader that's already "mated."  Sigh.

With no intention of apologizing about it, either.

Are there many extropians in Seattle (I've noticed a couple UW
domain posters, and I know several lurkers because we hang out anyway)?
Anyone planning on visiting any time soon?  A Seattle/Portland/Vancouver
area meeting wouldn't be completely infeasible. . .

Not to discourage Garrett Goebel, there are vegan archives of recipes
which you can find via rec.food.veg.  In fact, the contents of several
of these archives were posted over the last month.

Robin Hanson's altint list sounds fascinating; I for one hope it
gets off the ground soon so I can subscribe.  Can't say that I
have a site, but I have a friend with a NeXT on the 'net who may
like this idea; I'll ask.

In the meantime, the idea behind the list ties in nicely with one aspect of
anarchocapitalism I'm been toying with since reading MoF:  there is no
reason why enclaves practicing you-name-it-brand-of-political-economic-
philosophy can't exist within an anarchocapitalist society (a situation
resembling that depicted by Vinge in "The  Ungoverned"), sort
of a special case of protection rackets (or an extended case of a
family, depending on the institution and your point of view).  While
most on this list might not be thrilled by the prospect of worrying
about the social democrats next door in an extropian utopia,
elaborating on how this could peacably occur has some outreach
potential.  "Anarchocapitalism:  something for everyone."  OK, maybe
not.  :-)


