From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: Gott vs Barrow

   Here is a sampling of the reaction generated by Gott's paper in sci.space.
Every single response was negative except one. (One poster went as far as
comparing his work to numerology.) This goes to show that non-extropians
reach the same conclusions we do. (without such a philosophically 
biased reasoning for dismissing it). Gott's work had nothing to do with the
Fermi paradox -- it was about computing the length of existence of a species
based on its history. When applied to humans, there are holes big enough
in this theory to drive a asteroid sized brain through.

   CAP's conclusions are completely different -- CAP deals with
the universe as a whole and data that we already have. Gott predicts
the future, CAP explains the past and present.

   I don't see the acceptance of CAP and dismissal of Gott as extropian bias.
One is a well constructed plausible theory, the other is a rather poorly
constructed predictive tool.  Pretty clear cut to me. One crackpot
indicator is that Gott applied his algorithm to the Berlin wall and
it correctly predicted it's fall in the 90s. Gee, I wonder if I could use
it to predict the length of the next extropians flame?


